  "slug": "recovery-model-2949",
  "summary": "Recent preprints pair neural surrogates, graph models and shape descriptors with physiological simulation benchmarks and parameter recovery.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of machine learning to extract or validate physiological information from simulated and measured dynamics. The bile acid preprint is the most direct case: a Bayesian pipeline combining timescale model reduction and neural surrogates infers enterohepatic circulation parameters, and temporal blood sampling is reported to improve that recovery. GraphEP supplies 732 calibrated cardiac electrophysiology simulations and benchmarks graph neural networks against physiology-informed models. Topological Pharmacokinetics takes a model-free route, applying delay embedding and persistent homology to extract shape descriptors of concentration-time curves. The remaining items, SOSM survival geometry, OT-tSNE batch-integrated visualization and velotest assessment of RNA velocity embeddings, are methodologically adjacent rather than physiological model work. What is new is the shift toward model-free or surrogate-accelerated descriptors that are evaluated against explicit recovery or benchmark criteria.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
